 Newest first. Each entry records a call the supervisor made on the record of a reviewers' split or a resolution round (Merge-Verification-Policy, Resolution round), and the reasoning that settled it.
 
+## 2026-11-16 BUG-045: the help exemption read by position, and the line drawn on the guard's git reading after round 4 (R-082)
+
+- **Question:** how far BUG-045's fix reaches. The defect (docs/debug/BUG-045.md): a `-h` or `--help` word anywhere among git's words exempted every git refusal of the permission guard, so `git commit --no-verify -a -m -h` committed with the hooks skipped. Each round's reviews then found gaps older than BUG-045 in the readings the fix stands on: round 1, a git global option the guard did not know hid the subcommand, and an inline alias for a hook skipper passed; round 2, alias values read by their first raw word, a dotted alias key, abbreviated `--no-verify`, `--mirror` and lease options, and `git config --comment --get` read as a read; round 3, the push options' abbreviation floor (`--m` is `--mirror`) and short clusters holding a digit with `f`.
+- **Decision: each family met a second time was made whole, not extended, and the line is drawn at round 4.** The help exemption holds only where git can do nothing but print help (exactly one help word after the subcommand, or `worktree remove` and one help word); git's global options are read from a known list and any other one is refused as unreadable; every alias defined in the command (a word beginning `alias.`, in any case, holding `=`) is refused; a refused long option is read by any prefix git accepts (`--no-verify` from 6 characters on the hook skippers, `--mirror` from 3 and `--force` with its lease options from 4 on push), and a short cluster holding `f` with the digits `4` or `6` is a force push; `git config` naming `core.hooksPath` passes only in its three read forms and the bridge's own write, `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`. Round 4's reviews found nothing blocking; their two advisories (`-fo1`-style clusters, a `-c remote.<name>.mirror` setting) behave the same on round 3's code and go to docs/HARDENING.md as leads, with the earlier ones (`include.path`, `git config alias.<x>`, `--shallow-file` over-refused).
+- **Reasoning.** Rounds 3 and 4 are above the ceiling, each lifted by its class line (a defeated safety mechanism; round 4 also the brief's own error). Each finding was inside the guard's threat-model row: a refused action named in the command's own words behind an option, an abbreviation or an alias the guard reads. The supervisor's working rule for the tail (a second round on the same reader changes the design or draws the line) turned each list that kept growing into a rule a later git cannot reopen (a known option list, every inline alias refused, every accepted prefix read). What remains reaches the refused setting through a file or configuration written earlier, not the command's own words: outside the row. Told to the owner as a change they can stop (rule 58).
+
+## 2026-11-16 TASK-063: the round-3 call on Review B's `-- --help` finding corrected (R-082)
+
+- **Question:** whether the call recorded for Review B's rejection of TASK-063's round 3 stands. Review B (gpt-5.6-sol-high) had rejected on `git worktree remove -- --help`, graded a defeated safety mechanism; the supervisor recorded it as needing a contrived layout (no bridge layout names a worktree `--help`) and resolved the split for Review A's approval; the increment merged (PR #171).
+- **Decision: the call was wrong; the defect was BUG-045's, fixed by its own brief (PR #173).** Review A of the records pull request (#172, closed unmerged) named an operator of the same defect in ordinary use, `git commit --no-verify -a -m -h`, and the supervisor reproduced it on git: a commit with the hooks skipped, its subject `-h`.
+- **Reasoning.** decide.md judges the class, not the operator that exposed it: the exemption read a help word anywhere among git's words, and the worktree named `--help` was one operator of that defect. The supervisor judged the operator. The lesson stands under TASK-063 in docs/HARDENING.md: a finding at the ceiling is judged contrived only after its class has been looked for in ordinary forms.
+
 ## 2026-11-16 TASK-063: the guard's threat model drawn after round 9, its deliberate reaches recorded (R-082)
 
 - **Question:** whether round 9's findings that reach a refused action by a program that runs what it is handed send the work back. The security audit failed round 9 on ssh's `-o` rule stepped round (a getopt cluster, a leading blank, a quoted key, scp's and sftp's `-o`) and a locale value with a modifier ending `.utf8`; Review A named the same ssh forms and a `/bin/../` path to Git's launcher, beside two findings of another kind (B1, a regression of round 9 in the walk's time; B2, `NAME+=value` read as the command word). Rounds 5 to 9 had each graded such reaches blocking, and each round's audit had found the next program.
