+# BUG-045 round 2 report
+
+## Files changed
+
+- `bridge/cursor-bridge/permission-guard.py`
+- `bridge/cursor-bridge/MANIFEST.json` (regenerated by `python tools/make-manifest.py` in the Done run; not edited by hand)
+- `tests/test_bug045.py`
+- `docs/BUILDER_NOTES.md`
+- `handoff/BUG-045-r2-report.md`
+
+## Summary
+
+The permission guard now reads git's global options from a known list and refuses any other dash word before the subcommand as unreadable, and it refuses an alias defined in the command whose value's first word is a hook-skipping subcommand other than push. Round 1's lone-help exemption is unchanged. The new cases sit below round 1's builder cases in `tests/test_bug045.py`.
+
+## Causes
+
+An unknown git global option no longer hides the subcommand: `git_subcommand` reports a dash word outside the known list as unreadable instead of skipping it, the git branch of `_check_word_list` refuses that command with `_cannot` naming the option, and `state_changing` returns True for it.
+An alias defined in the command for a hook-skipping subcommand no longer passes: the alias reading refuses a value whose first word is `commit`, `merge`, `rebase`, `am`, `cherry-pick` or `revert`, with the existing alias refusal text, while `push` keeps the forced-push test it already had.
+
+## F3
+
+`git_subcommand` (lines 2437-2454) skips only the known list held beside `HOOK_SKIPPERS` (lines 106-109): a value-taking option consumes the next word, an `=` form consumes that one word, and a flag consumes itself; any other word that begins with `-` returns `(_GIT_UNREADABLE, word)`. The git branch of `_check_word_list` (lines 2330-2332), after the alias loop (lines 2325-2329) and before the five refusals that read `sub`, refuses that with `_cannot`, cutting the option at 60 characters. `state_changing` (lines 2411-2414) returns True for the same result. Bare `--exec-path` is a flag, so the next word is the subcommand. Bare `--config-env` is still refused in `_walk_to_command` (lines 673-674).
+`git --help` printed this usage line (the command's full output is below): `usage: git [-v | --version] [-h | --help] [-C <path>] [-c <name>=<value>] [--exec-path[=<path>]] [--html-path] [--man-path] [--info-path] [-p | --paginate | -P | --no-pager] [--no-replace-objects] [--no-lazy-fetch] [--no-optional-locks] [--no-advice] [--bare] [--git-dir=<path>] [--work-tree=<path>] [--namespace=<name>] [--config-env=<name>=<envvar>] <command> [<args>]`.
+Options that usage line shows and the ruled list lacked: none.
+The ruled list keeps these, which that usage line does not show: the separate-word forms `--git-dir`, `--work-tree` and `--namespace` (the usage line shows only the `=` forms); `--super-prefix` and `--super-prefix=`; `--attr-source` and `--attr-source=`; `--literal-pathspecs`, `--glob-pathspecs`, `--noglob-pathspecs` and `--icase-pathspecs`; `--list-cmds=`. None of them was removed.
+
+```
+usage: git [-v | --version] [-h | --help] [-C <path>] [-c <name>=<value>]
+           [--exec-path[=<path>]] [--html-path] [--man-path] [--info-path]
+           [-p | --paginate | -P | --no-pager] [--no-replace-objects] [--no-lazy-fetch]
+           [--no-optional-locks] [--no-advice] [--bare] [--git-dir=<path>]
+           [--work-tree=<path>] [--namespace=<name>] [--config-env=<name>=<envvar>]
+           <command> [<args>]
+
+These are common Git commands used in various situations:
+
+start a working area (see also: git help tutorial)
+   clone      Clone a repository into a new directory
+   init       Create an empty Git repository or reinitialize an existing one
+
+work on the current change (see also: git help everyday)
+   add        Add file contents to the index
+   mv         Move or rename a file, a directory, or a symlink
+   restore    Restore working tree files
+   rm         Remove files from the working tree and from the index
+
+examine the history and state (see also: git help revisions)
+   bisect     Use binary search to find the commit that introduced a bug
+   diff       Show changes between commits, commit and working tree, etc
+   grep       Print lines matching a pattern
+   log        Show commit logs
+   show       Show various types of objects
+   status     Show the working tree status
+
+grow, mark and tweak your common history
+   backfill   Download missing objects in a partial clone
+   branch     List, create, or delete branches
+   commit     Record changes to the repository
+   merge      Join two or more development histories together
+   rebase     Reapply commits on top of another base tip
+   reset      Set `HEAD` or the index to a known state
+   switch     Switch branches
+   tag        Create, list, delete or verify tags
+
+collaborate (see also: git help workflows)
+   fetch      Download objects and refs from another repository
+   pull       Fetch from and integrate with another repository or a local branch
+   push       Update remote refs along with associated objects
+
+'git help -a' and 'git help -g' list available subcommands and some
+concept guides. See 'git help <command>' or 'git help <concept>'
+to read about a specific subcommand or concept.
+See 'git help git' for an overview of the system.
+```
+
+## F4
+
+`_alias_value_prefix` (lines 2014-2042) still finds the value start after `=`, blanks and one optional quote, still returns True for `!` and for `_ALIAS_PREFIXES` (`worktree`, `pr`, `push`, `api`) under the same boundary, and now also returns True when the value's first whitespace-delimited word is `commit`, `merge`, `rebase`, `am`, `cherry-pick` or `revert` (compared in lower case). `_alias_names_refused` (lines 2045-2055) is unchanged: it still refuses when that helper returns True, and it still applies the `worktree remove`, `pr merge` and forced-push tests, so the refusal text stays `a git alias that defines a refused command (%s)`. `push` is not in the new six; an alias whose value starts with `push` is still refused by the existing prefix.
+
+## F5
+
+Each new case is below round 1's three builder cases. Mutations by hand: none.
+
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[dash-C]` pins `-C sub` before `status` passing and the same option before `commit --no-verify -m x` refused with `--no-verify` in the reason.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[dash-c]` pins `-c color.ui=never` (neither `core.hooksPath` nor an `alias.` key) the same way.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[git-dir]` pins `--git-dir .git` the same way.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[work-tree]` pins `--work-tree .` the same way.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[namespace]` pins `--namespace ns` the same way.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[super-prefix]` pins `--super-prefix x/` the same way.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[attr-source]` pins `--attr-source HEAD` the same way.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[git-dir-equals]` pins `--git-dir=.git` the same way.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[work-tree-equals]` pins `--work-tree=.` the same way.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[namespace-equals]` pins `--namespace=ns` the same way.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[super-prefix-equals]` pins `--super-prefix=x/` the same way.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[attr-source-equals]` pins `--attr-source=HEAD` the same way.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[exec-path-equals]` pins `--exec-path=/usr/bin` the same way.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[list-cmds-equals]` pins `--list-cmds=main` the same way; git would list commands and run nothing, so the commit refusal is a safe over-refusal.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[dash-p]` pins `-p` the same way.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[paginate]` pins `--paginate` the same way.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[dash-P]` pins `-P` the same way.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[no-pager]` pins `--no-pager` the same way.
+- `test_bug045_a_known_git_global_option_is_read[bare]` pins `--bare` the same way.
